\newlabel{sec:embedding}{{2.1}{6}{The proposed Ultra-LSNT framework}{equation.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Ultra-LSNT architecture. The input tensor is decomposed into a smooth trend component and a high-frequency seasonal component. Multi-scale embeddings on the seasonal branch use parallel Conv1D kernels ($k=3$, $k=7$) to capture short-term dynamics and medium-term patterns. In the sparse MoE encoder, the gating network activates Top-$K$ experts (dark solid routes) while non-selected routes are faded dashed lines. A Jump Gate with condition $I_t < \tau $ dispatches tokens to a red compute path (through MoE) or a green skip path (bypassing MoE). Trend and MoE outputs are merged by Add/Concat to produce the final forecast $\hat  {\mathbf  {Y}}$.}}{7}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:architecture}{{1}{7}{Ultra-LSNT architecture. The input tensor is decomposed into a smooth trend component and a high-frequency seasonal component. Multi-scale embeddings on the seasonal branch use parallel Conv1D kernels ($k=3$, $k=7$) to capture short-term dynamics and medium-term patterns. In the sparse MoE encoder, the gating network activates Top-$K$ experts (dark solid routes) while non-selected routes are faded dashed lines. A Jump Gate with condition $I_t < \tau $ dispatches tokens to a red compute path (through MoE) or a green skip path (bypassing MoE). Trend and MoE outputs are merged by Add/Concat to produce the final forecast $\hat {\mathbf {Y}}$}{figure.caption.4}{}}
